Modern Apple Silicon build
==========================

FontForge's font engine is portable C/C++. Its UI is an in-house abstraction
called `gdraw`; on macOS the GDK3 backend runs on the Cocoa/Quartz window
system, with Objective-C integration for the pasteboard, Apple Events,
document opening and launchd startup. XQuartz is only needed for old
pre-2019 X11 releases; current releases do not require it.

A SwiftUI port is not required for Apple Silicon. A full SwiftUI port would
mean replacing `gdraw` and the dialogs built on it, which is a separate
rewrite rather than a build or packaging change. The practical path is to
keep the C engine and improve the macOS shell incrementally.

Build natively on an Apple Silicon Mac with Homebrew:

    ./osx/build-macos-arm64.sh

The script defaults the deployment target to the running macOS version,
because current Homebrew dependencies are built for recent macOS releases.
Set `MACOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=11.0` to target the oldest Apple Silicon macOS,
provided the dependency versions allow it.

Endpoint security tools may reject ad-hoc signed bundles after `lddx`
relocates the dependencies. Pass a Developer ID identity to codesign the
finished bundle with the hardened runtime and a secure timestamp, both of
which are required for notarization:

    FONTFORGE_CODESIGN_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: ..." \
        ./osx/build-macos-arm64.sh

The rest of this file documents the legacy MacPorts workflow.

The main Portfile to build with macports is included here. There has
become a little post processing step and the make-package.sh simply
uses the create-osx-app-bundle.sh script to create an app.zip file
from the macports built sources.

As of January 2015, the build is performed using the following script
sequence. The create-osx-app-bundle.sh script will create
~/FontForge.app.dmg which you can then archive and upload that
file as desired. The script which does the upload is not in this
repository as it includes some authentication credentials and is
specific to the current distribution site.

Before this starts, change to the directory
/usr/local/src/github-fontforge
and clone the fontforge sources into there:
git clone git@github.com:fontforge/fontforge.git fontforge
Add the aforementioned path to /opt/local/etc/macports/sources.conf as
file:///usr/local/src/github-fontforge
above the default MacPorts sources.

cd /usr/local/src/github-fontforge
sudo portindex
cd fontforge/osx
./force-rebuild.sh
cd /opt/local/var/macports/build/_usr_local_src_github-fontforge_fontforge_osx/fontforge/work/fontforge-2.0.0_beta1
bash ./osx/create-osx-app-bundle.sh

You will also want to configure your macports environment to include
in binaries so that the link paths can be modified for packaged
execution.

For example, the file
/opt/local/share/macports/Tcl/port1.0/portconfigure.tcl default
configure.ldflags {"-L${prefix}/lib -Wl,-headerpad_max_install_names"}

